From: Substrate
Subject: [PATCH] lib/iconv.c: export plain iconv/iconv_open/iconv_close on substrate

GNU libiconv normally exports only the libiconv-prefixed symbols and relies on
its <iconv.h> macro rename (iconv->libiconv) so consumers link the prefixed
names.  Substrate has no iconv in libc, so libiconv IS the system iconv — but
some ports (ksh93 / libast) ship their own non-renaming <iconv.h> and reference
the plain iconv/iconv_open/iconv_close names, which then fail to link.

The library already provides plain-name strong aliases for exactly this case,
guarded for FreeBSD (where libiconv is the native iconv).  Extend that guard to
substrate, and give the aliases explicit default ELF visibility so they survive
the library's -fvisibility=hidden build and are actually exported from
libiconv.so (without this they appear only in the static archive).

--- a/lib/iconv.c
+++ b/lib/iconv.c
@@ -612,11 +612,11 @@

 #endif

-#if defined __FreeBSD__ && !defined __gnu_freebsd__
+#if (defined __FreeBSD__ || defined __substrate__) && !defined __gnu_freebsd__
 /* GNU libiconv is the native FreeBSD iconv implementation since 2002.
    It wants to define the symbols 'iconv_open', 'iconv', 'iconv_close'.  */
 #define strong_alias(name, aliasname) _strong_alias(name, aliasname)
 #define _strong_alias(name, aliasname) \
-  extern __typeof (name) aliasname __attribute__ ((alias (#name)));
+  extern __typeof (name) aliasname __attribute__ ((alias (#name), __visibility__ ("default")));
 #undef iconv_open
 #undef iconv
 #undef iconv_close
